    deepTitle: "Pacific wine coast",
    deep: [
      "Dominican mission geography, not Aztec cities, framed northern Baja’s colonial map. Nuestra Señora de Guadalupe (1834) sat in the valley that later grew grapes; Ensenada itself lacked that mission birth and so argues over 1882 as its civic birthday. José Manuel Ruiz’s 1806 ranch at the bay passed to the Gastélum family, who grazed it. The 1882 capital decree, pushed by customs officials who wanted the port as head of the northern district, is what urban historians now treat as founding.",
      "Mining booms in the Juárez and surrounding ranges pulled labor through the port, then faded. The Revolution interrupted growth, and the 1915 capital move to Mexicali left Ensenada a small town. What returned was cross-border leisure: bars, hotels, and later the malecón, thickened by U.S. Prohibition traffic. Santo Tomás Valley and San Vicente also grow grapes, but Valle de Guadalupe became the tasting-room landscape because its inland heat and night cooling sit just beyond the marine layer’s reach. That 30 km is the whole microclimate lesson.",
      "Molokan settlers farmed without a wine vocation; several families later opened museums and cafes as descendants remaining after many relatives moved to California. Large Mexican houses — L.A. Cetto, Santo Tomás, Domecq — industrialised volume from mid-century onward. Boutique labels multiplied after 2000, along with hotels that treat water as an amenity. Fiestas de la Vendimia in August mark harvest in a climate that still gets most of its rain in winter. Olives and cattle remain on the same valley floor as the vines.",
      "Chaparral and coastal sage on the hills, irrigated vines on the valley floor, and a foggy waterfront are three climates in one municipality. Drought is the ceiling for gardens and for the aquifer that Ensenada and the valley already share. Summer on the coast is cool enough for a sweater at night; inland it is a heat-and-cool swing that ripens Tempranillo and Cabernet. Relocation here is a bet on Pacific upwelling continuing to buffer a desert peninsula, and on wells that are not infinite.",
    ],
